
<p class="wp-block-paragraph">We got news that Apple has settled the <a href="https://www.apple.com/newsroom/2026/08/apple-announces-changes-for-apps-in-the-european-union/">App Store fee dispute with the European Union</a>, which is good. If Apple can get out of this mess with a negotiated deal instead of <a href="https://9to5mac.com/2025/07/07/apple-formally-appeals-e500-million-dma-fine-in-the-eu/">years of litigation and penalties</a>, they should consider themselves ahead of the game.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">When I first saw the headline, however, I was hoping it meant a more global agreement with the European Union. I talk to European Apple enthusiasts every day in the MacSparky Labs, and they are all incredibly frustrated that Apple fails to <a href="https://www.apple.com/newsroom/2026/06/due-to-dma-siri-ai-delayed-in-eu-for-ios-27-and-ipados-27/">release features in the EU that are available elsewhere</a>.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">I&#8217;m not going to pretend to know who&#8217;s to blame, but European citizens and Apple customers are the ones paying the price.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Back in my days as a lawyer, I was a party to enough ongoing negotiations to know that the people outside the negotiations have no clue what&#8217;s actually going on. But I can hope this is merely a first step and that my European Apple-using friends can get access to Siri AI sooner rather than later.</p>
